.TH GIT-HOTSPOTS 1 "May 2026" "git-hotspots 0.1.0-alpha.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-hotspots \- deterministic local Git-history hotspot prompts
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-hotspots
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.br
.B git-hotspots --explain
.br
.B git-hotspots --version
.br
.B git-hotspots --help
|
.B -h
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B git-hotspots
analyses a local Git worktree and emits deterministic file-level hotspot
investigation prompts. Hotspots are prompts from local Git-history evidence, not
bug predictions, developer rankings, maintainer judgement, productivity
analytics, or objective code-quality scores.
.PP
Runtime defaults are local-first. The alpha reads local Git history and never
fetches, pushes, uploads source, contacts remotes, or emits telemetry.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --repo " PATH"
Local Git worktree to analyse. The default is the current directory.
.TP
.BI --limit " N"
Maximum ranked files to emit. The default is 10.
.TP
.BI --format " table|json|markdown"
Report format. The default is table.
.TP
.BI --since " REV"
Analyse commits after REV through HEAD.
.TP
.BI --scope " all|project"
Select the evidence universe. The alpha default is
.B project,
which excludes the literal repo-root prefixes
.BR .flow/ ,
.BR .zig-cache/ ,
.BR zig-out/ ,
.BR target/ ,
.BR node_modules/ ,
.BR dist/ ,
.BR build/ ,
and
.B coverage/
before scoring.
.B all
uses the full local Git-history evidence universe.
.TP
.BI --include-prefix " PATH"
Repeatable repo-relative literal Git path prefix to include before scoring. This
narrows the evidence universe and is not a glob.
.TP
.BI --exclude-prefix " PATH"
Repeatable repo-relative literal Git path prefix to exclude before scoring. Use
slash separators. Excludes win over includes.
.TP
.BI --inspect " PATH"
Exact repo-relative file drilldown within the selected scope. It selects one
file after scoring and ranking, before normal
.B --limit
truncation. Accepted in-scope Git rename aliases may resolve to the canonical
path. Use the Git-style
.B \et
escape to target a tab in a path.
.TP
.B --symbols
Add opt-in current working-tree Tree-sitter symbol evidence for Zig, Go,
Python, JavaScript, Lua, Rust, TypeScript, or TSX files among retained ranked
hotspots. With
.B --inspect PATH,
add symbols only for the inspected file. Symbol evidence does not affect score,
rank, lineage, confidence, co-change evidence, or file-level evidence.
.TP
.B --symbol-line-history
With
.B --symbols,
add opt-in current-line Git evidence for current supported symbol line ranges at
HEAD. This is not true symbol history, lineage, scoring, or ownership.
.TP
.B --historical-symbols
With
.B --symbols,
add bounded true historical hunk attribution for retained ranked-file
candidates. This is not semantic symbol lineage, reference/use analysis,
ownership, bug prediction, scoring replacement, or a ranking input.
.TP
.B --symbol-relationships
With
.B --symbols,
add bounded local relationship evidence for retained ranked-file candidates.
This is caveated provider evidence, not call-graph truth, dependency proof,
ownership, developer metrics, bug prediction, scoring replacement, or a ranking
input.
.TP
.BI --symbol-limit " N"
With
.B --symbols,
limit human table and Markdown current, historical, and relationship rows. JSON
item arrays remain complete within internal bounds. The default is 25.
.TP
.B --progress
Write opt-in coarse analysis progress to stderr for long runs. Progress does not
change table, JSON, or Markdown reports.
.TP
.B --explain
Print the current scoring semantics without analysing a repository.
.TP
.B --version
Print the git-hotspots version without analysing a repository.
.TP
.BR -h ", " --help
Print help without analysing a repository.
.SH EXAMPLES
Inspect the local checkout:
.PP
.EX
git-hotspots --repo . --limit 10 --format table
git-hotspots --repo . --format json
git-hotspots --repo . --format markdown
.EE
.PP
Use an exploratory project scope with progress:
.PP
.EX
git-hotspots --repo . --scope project --since HEAD~500 --progress --format markdown
.EE
.PP
Optionally add current symbol evidence for ranked files or one inspected file:
.PP
.EX
git-hotspots --repo . --symbols --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --inspect src/main.zig --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --inspect src/main.zig --symbols --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --symbols --symbol-line-history --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --inspect src/main.zig --symbols --symbol-line-history --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --symbols --historical-symbols --format markdown
git-hotspots --repo . --symbols --symbol-relationships --format markdown
.EE
.PP
Use the static scoring explanation:
.PP
.EX
git-hotspots --explain
.EE
.SH REPORT SEMANTICS
The score combines frequency, churn, recency, and co-change evidence. Confidence
summarises available evidence and caveats. Co-change means files changed in the
same commits; it is not dependency analysis.
.PP
Git-detected file renames are folded conservatively when both old and new paths
are in scope. This is file-path lineage from local Git history only, not symbol
or function lineage, semantic ownership, bug prediction, quality scoring, or
developer ranking.
.PP
Reports may include caveats for shallow history, partial or promisor history,
dirty worktrees, binary or non-text churn, large commits, rename lineage,
scope-partial lineage, and paths deleted or missing at HEAD.
.SH PRIVACY AND LOCAL-FIRST CAVEATS
Runtime defaults are local-first. The alpha does not fetch more history, contact
remotes, upload source, emit telemetry, or rely on runtime AI judgement for
hotspot truth.
.PP
Public report output uses repo-relative paths and avoids author identities,
absolute local paths, remotes, raw source snippets, and commit messages. Shallow
or partial history is reported as a caveat instead of being repaired by network
access.
.SH PROVIDER BOUNDARIES
Provider evidence is optional current-file enrichment for retained ranked files
or for one inspected path. The supported lanes are Zig, Go, Python, JavaScript,
Lua, Rust, TypeScript, and TSX. Unsupported files preserve file-level evidence
and are counted without parser diagnostics.
.PP
Rust support does not evaluate Cargo, crates, module resolution, macro expansion
output, cfg feature selection, type checking, dependency graphs, or semantic
Rust analysis.
.PP
Providers do not evaluate packages, generated-source policy, Node or TypeScript
configuration, build tags, runtime modules, metatables, React or DOM semantics.
They make no true symbol history, semantic symbol lineage, reference/use
analysis, scoring, or ownership claims.
.PP
.B --historical-symbols
uses local historical blobs and hunks for retained ranked-file candidates only.
It reports revision-local symbol or fallback aggregates with caveats and bounded
sample commits; it does not checkout files, fetch history, upload source, or
change ranking.
.PP
.B --symbol-relationships
uses bounded local provider output for retained ranked-file candidates in the
Python, JavaScript, TypeScript, and TSX lanes only. It reports source and target
endpoints, unresolved targets, provider identity, freshness, failure,
confidence, caveats, record bounds, and omitted counts; it does not prove
calls, dependencies, ownership, developer metrics, bugs, or ranking changes.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Invalid CLI arguments exit 2 with concise stderr diagnostics that name the
failed flag and, when deterministic, include a valid next command shape. For
example,
.B --symbol-line-history
and
.B --historical-symbols
require
.B --symbols.
.B --symbol-relationships
also requires
.B --symbols.
Use
.B git-hotspots --help
or
.B git-hotspots -h
for the repository-independent option reference.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B git-hotspots
exits 0 after printing help, version, explain output, or a successful report. It
exits 1 for analysis failures such as non-Git repositories, bare repositories,
empty repositories, invalid revisions, or inspect targets missing from the
selected scope. It exits 2 for invalid CLI arguments.
